#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" :question/math-academy-id "e-4823" :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}$. Since we are told that $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-1)_(⏟)_(x_{1})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + (3)_(⏟)_(x_{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841f498b-f737-5086-8f79-44f1a143bebb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "6c7237cd-c655-575f-83ac-7a0891c336b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4824" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-3)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} =-5; - 4x_{2} = 4\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left(-1 \\right) = -5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1 = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6a6ea8-6100-5224-b75f-b87c037fafbb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing More Than Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4825" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} + x_{3}b_{3} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{1}{3})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 1; - 9x_{2} - x_{3} = 7; \\frac{4}{3}x_{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "a79126f2-e26c-5929-93d8-825d85b9a438" :question/math-academy-id "e-6282" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = (-5) \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_1 + 2 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841f498b-f737-5086-8f79-44f1a143bebb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8139155f-b963-50a4-970f-d8c333ba3c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118186} {:answer/id #uuid "e239d86c-e73f-560d-9af6-f583173bd251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118187} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd4ec06-6cd8-5f96-af98-952e984adef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118188} {:answer/id #uuid "1beaa329-d937-5bd4-b403-82541c802fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∉ Span{b_{1}, b_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118189} {:answer/id #uuid "20610432-7e80-562c-9f3e-0fab989693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e239d86c-e73f-560d-9af6-f583173bd251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118187} :answer-field/id #uuid "408eef1d-d869-52d5-b495-ac0345927cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118185}] :question/id #uuid "f17a8e42-194f-52ed-9136-123dd348b4bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-34617" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where\n\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$?"}]}
